Commentary / interpretation
    • Power: With identical battery current limits, DE5 yields more mechanical power because we used an optimistic higher efficiency (94% vs 80%). At B1 continuous, that’s ~400 W extra mechanical for the DE5 case (2.7 kW vs 2.3 kW). At B2 continuous, difference is larger in absolute terms (9.0 kW vs 7.7 kW).
    • Range: For equal pack capacity (assumed 480 and 960 Wh), range is independent of motor efficiency if energy-per-km already accounts for system losses; but if you instead compute range from mechanical needs, motor efficiency matters. Using the simple battery-Wh/Wh-per-km approach above, ranges are 24 km / 48 km. (If you reduce P_loss and raise motor efficiency, you can get more miles per Wh; I can compute that variant if you want mechanical-power-based range.)
    • Weight impact: DE5 assumed slightly heavier motor (4.0 vs 3.5 kg), so total system mass increases ~0.5 kg; but because DE5 produces more mechanical power for the same battery, the specific power (W/kg) is superior for DE5 in these assumptions (423 vs 391 W/kg at B1).
    • Thermal margin & currents: The continuous current values (60 A and 100 A) are realistic for mid/high-power e-bikes. The DE5 advantage will be reduced if its real efficiency is lower than assumed or if copper losses / thermal limits force current reduction. Also note battery capacity assumption is crucial for range.

