Practical takeaways & recommendations
    1. DE5 advantage in this rough comparison stems mainly from higher motor efficiency. If the DE5 prototype can sustain 90–95% real-world efficiency including inverter losses and at real operating points, it will deliver higher usable power and better specific power. However:
        ◦ High model efficiencies you reported (0.99+) are optimistic; use conservative 90–95% when planning hardware.
        ◦ Thermal and magnetic saturation behaviors can reduce peak and continuous capabilities; verify with thermal/FEM and saturation-aware tests.
    2. Battery sizing vs current limits: B1 is a modest e-bike pack (2.9 kW continuous); that’s already sporting — continuous mechanical power in the 2.3–2.7 kW range is far beyond typical legal e-bike assistance (250–750 W), so for legal e-bikes you’ll throttle down. But for cargo or speed pedelec style rigs, these numbers are useful.
    3. Weight trade: small increase in motor mass is usually acceptable if efficiency and torque density increase meaningfully — confirm with mechanical packaging analysis.
    4. Control & protection: because the DE5 topology can produce higher torque per amp (k_t), you should re-evaluate current limits (I_bus_limit) and use energy buffering (caps) to handle short transients rather than rely on battery peak rating only. (I can produce cap sizing code next.)


1. Why “more available power” can increase range
Efficiency for electric motors is not flat — it’s typically a bell-shaped curve versus torque (or load).
    • At very low torque, losses are dominated by iron/core and inverter switching losses → efficiency drops.
    • At very high torque (near rated current), copper ( I^2R ) losses dominate → efficiency also drops.
    • Somewhere around 40–70 % of rated load, most motors reach their peak efficiency.
So if a BLDC hub is barely big enough for the job (i.e., the trip speed/power demand ≈ its rated power), it operates deep in the high-current, low-efficiency region the entire time.
By contrast, your DE5 1×1 module, having higher torque and power overhead, will supply the same required wheel power at a lower internal current density and better field alignment, keeping it near the crest of its η-curve.

